{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYC5Lr82neEPgr7M1cF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Google's announcement of Gemini 4 Carbon spends as much space on how the model behaves during long agentic work as on its scores. Google says its teams have seen Carbon run for days on multi-step engineering tasks, such as root-causing and patching subtle bugs in large codebases and low-level systems. A job that long is hard to supervise step by step, so the failure modes Google chose to measure are the ones that could go unnoticed for hours.\n\nThe first is shortcut-taking. On Terminal-Bench 4.0, Google reports that successful cheating fell to 0.0% and attempts to cheat to 0.4%, while overall accuracy improved. In live coding environments with its internal teams, it says high-severity misalignment alerts dropped by almost 80%. The second is unsupported claims. In internal testing, Google says Carbon cuts them by roughly half compared with Gemini 4 Argon, and that it either writes methodological caveats into the deliverable or withholds a blocked action and lists what is still required. The third is scope. Google reports that overreach fell by 26% and under-service by 29%, and that when sources conflict, Carbon retrieves them and surfaces the discrepancies to the user before acting.\n\nFor an agent that reads untrusted content during a multi-day task, prompt injection matters too. On Gray Swan's Indirect Prompt Injection benchmark, Google says Carbon's attack success rate is 0.0%, compared with 0.7% for Argon, meaning it resisted every attack attempt in that evaluation.\n\nAll of these figures come from Google's own testing or from a benchmark Google reports on, and the article gives no absolute baselines for several of the percentage changes. A 0.0% result describes a fixed set of attacks or tasks, not a guarantee about every case. Google's statement that users \"should observe a much better collaborative experience\" is an expectation, and nobody outside the initial group of cyber defenders can check it yet, since Carbon has not been released to developers, businesses or consumers. Whether the reductions in fabrication and overreach hold up in other people's long-running work is the open question."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1858,"cache_read_input_tokens":3857,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1858},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a02ec77-7c98-442b-8b74-e852d342f3ad","uuid":"8fc1b09a-f3ad-4050-acb4-a8cbb2ef2074","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:20:28.912Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYC4TmBeTLqdwNn8egF"}
